# {{DS_REPO}}

Persistent context repo for Claude sessions working on the {{SYSTEM_NAME}} design system ({{PROJECT_NAME}} / {{PARENT_ORG}} {{DOMAIN}}).

> **Scope: design system only.** Tokens, styles, components, variants, bindings, the manifest, skills
> docs, publish, drift, and the spiels mechanism. Screen building + coded prototypes live in a
> **separate context/repo** (`{{STUDIO_REPO}}`) that consumes the published {{SYSTEM_NAME}} library.

## Why this repo exists

Working with Claude across multiple sessions requires re-establishing context each time: which design system, which Figma file IDs, which conventions, which working preferences. Manual copy-paste is fragile and grows stale.

This repo solves that. A single bootstrap URL gives Claude everything it needs.

## How it works

1. Open this repo in **Claude Code** — it loads `CLAUDE.md` (→ `CONTEXT.md`) automatically on startup.
2. `CONTEXT.md` + `CLAUDE.md` carry the project facts, skill table, and critical rules; the `.claude/`
   **agents** (`ds-steward`, `component-designer`, `brand-designer`) + **commands**
   (`/design-system-check`, `/binding-audit`, `/drift-check`) carry the workflows.
3. Load `skills/*.md` per task and delegate to the right agent — no copy-paste, no scenario files.

When {{SYSTEM_NAME}} changes (new tokens, component updates, etc.), update the relevant skill files +
manifest here; the next session picks them up automatically. (`CONTEXT.md` can also be pasted into a
Claude.ai chat as a reference if you ever work outside Claude Code.)

## Repo structure

```
{{DS_REPO}}/
├── CONTEXT.md                       ← shared project facts, skill table + critical rules (read first)
├── CLAUDE.md                        ← Claude Code reads this on startup (if present)
├── README.md                        ← this file
├── DESIGN.md                        ← design principles + benchmarks (shared ground truth)
├── AGENTS-HANDOFF.md                ← agent orchestration model
├── skills/
│   ├── ds-tokens.md               ← variables, text styles, effect styles, VariableIDs
│   ├── ds-components.md            ← component keys, variants, conventions
│   ├── figma-gotchas.md        ← Plugin API traps and workarounds (NON-NEGOTIABLE)
│   ├── component-authoring-standards.md  ← component authoring gate
│   └── spiels.md               ← i18n string variable tokens
├── manifest/
│   └── manifest-{{DS_VERSION}}.json     ← current canonical baseline (older versions kept for diff)
├── tools/
│   ├── inventory-snippet.js    ← Figma plugin code to dump current {{SYSTEM_NAME}} state
│   └── doc-drift-checker.py    ← compare manifest vs actual (run via terminal)
├── docs/
│   └── sessions/                    ← chronological session notes (archive)
└── .claude/                         ← Claude Code agents + commands (the workflows)
    ├── agents/                      ds-steward.md · component-designer.md · brand-designer.md
    └── commands/                    design-system-check · binding-audit · drift-check
```

## Starting a session

**Claude Code (primary):** just open this repo — `CLAUDE.md` + `CONTEXT.md` load on startup and the
`.claude/` agents + commands are available. Tell Claude the task; it loads the matching `skills/*.md`
and delegates to `ds-steward` / `component-designer` / `brand-designer`.

**Claude.ai chat (optional reference):** paste this to load the context as reference. The workflows
live in the agents (which Claude.ai doesn't run), so drive the work manually from the skills:

```
Load my project context from:
https://raw.githubusercontent.com/{{GITHUB_OWNER}}/{{DS_REPO}}/main/CONTEXT.md

I'll tell you what we're working on next.
```

Private repo: append `?token=YOUR_PAT` (a GitHub PAT with `repo` scope) to the raw URL. Note: a
PAT-in-URL appears in chat history — for higher security use Claude Projects with manual file uploads.

## Roster

| Agent | Owns | Fires on |
|---|---|---|
| `ds-steward` | variables/styles authoring, token architecture, governance, the binding-audit GATE, publish hygiene, drift, manifest, skills/docs of record, spiels mechanism, lookup | "add a token/style", "audit bindings", "publish", "check drift", "update the skills", "what's the token/key for X?" |
| `component-designer` | component / component_set construction, variants, states, layout, visual craft, binding to tokens, a11y-at-build | "build/modify the X component", "add a variant", "fix the states/layout", "expose this slot" |
| `brand-designer` | identity direction, illustration style, imagery, photography system, video, marketing creative | "brand direction", "illustration style", "grade these photos", "set up the render" |

Plus three thin slash commands that delegate to `ds-steward`: `/design-system-check`,
`/binding-audit`, `/drift-check`.

## Team setup (onboarding a teammate)

The agents and commands are **project-scoped** — committed under `.claude/` in this repo — so there's
nothing to install or copy. Anyone who clones the repo and opens Claude Code from inside it gets the
3 agents, the 3 slash commands, `CLAUDE.md`, and the `skills/` docs automatically.

**Each teammate, once:**

1. **Install Claude Code** ([claude.com/claude-code](https://claude.com/claude-code)).
2. **Clone the repo and open Claude Code from inside it:**
   ```bash
   git clone https://github.com/{{GITHUB_OWNER}}/{{DS_REPO}}.git
   cd {{DS_REPO}}
   claude
   ```
   On startup Claude Code auto-loads `CLAUDE.md` and auto-discovers the `.claude/` agents
   (`ds-steward`, `component-designer`, `brand-designer`) + commands (`/design-system-check`,
   `/binding-audit`, `/drift-check`). No setup step — they just appear.
3. **Connect the MCP servers** the agents rely on (these are per-person, per-machine — they are *not*
   stored in the repo):
   - **Figma** — required for all source work (`use_figma`). The teammate needs edit access to the
     {{SYSTEM_NAME}} source file `{{DS_FIGMA_KEY}}`, and the Figma Desktop Bridge running.
   - **GitHub** — to commit context updates back.
   - Keep it minimal: connect only Figma + GitHub (+ Notion if briefs live there). Idle connectors
     burn context tokens and widen the prompt-injection surface.

**What does *not* travel with the repo (by design):**

- `.claude/settings.local.json` — personal permission allowlist + local config. Each person builds
  their own; it's intentionally uncommitted.
- MCP connections — set up per machine (step 3).

**Unchanged for the whole team:** only a **human can click "Publish Library"** in the Figma UI
(Critical Rule #1 — the Plugin API cannot publish). Source edits don't reach consumers until someone
publishes. Plan Mode is the hard gate before any source write or publish.

### Teammates without Claude Code (Claude.ai chat / free tier)

Claude Code — and therefore the agents + slash commands — needs a Pro or Max plan. A teammate on the
free tier, or who only uses the Claude.ai web chat, can still use this repo as a **reference** and drive
the work by hand:

1. In a Claude.ai chat, load the context — paste the `CONTEXT.md` raw-URL prompt from
   [*Starting a session*](#starting-a-session) above.
2. Open the relevant `skills/*.md` for the task and follow it manually. The agents don't execute in
   chat, so *you* play the `ds-steward` / `component-designer` / `brand-designer` roles — use
   [`AGENTS-HANDOFF.md`](AGENTS-HANDOFF.md) as the playbook for the build → audit → publish seam.
3. Writing to the Figma source still needs the **Figma connector + Desktop Bridge**, and the same
   human-only Publish rule applies. Without that connection, chat is **read-only**: great for looking
   up tokens/keys/specs, reviewing, and drafting copy — not for mutating the source.

## Updating the context

When {{SYSTEM_NAME}} changes happen:

1. Update the relevant `skills/*.md` file(s)
2. If tokens/styles/components changed, regenerate `manifest/manifest-vX.X.json`:
   - Run `tools/inventory-snippet.js` in a Figma `use_figma` call
   - Save output as `manifest/manifest-vX.X.json` (new version) or replace existing
3. Update the version number in `CONTEXT.md` (and `CLAUDE.md`) when a new {{SYSTEM_NAME}} publish happens
4. For mid-version fixes (no new publish, but spec correction), add a `patches[]` entry to the current manifest with date, scope, and gotcha cross-reference.
5. Run the binding audit on any new component built via `use_figma` — target zero unbound elements before publish
6. `git commit && git push`
7. Next Claude session sees the updates automatically

## Updating workflows

Workflows live in the `.claude/` agents (and the thin `.claude/commands/` that only delegate to them).
Edit the agent — never duplicate its logic into a command (that re-creates the drift this repo exists
to kill). See `AGENTS-HANDOFF.md` for the orchestration model.

## Drift detection

After each {{SYSTEM_NAME}} publish:

```bash
# 1. Run the inventory snippet in Figma against the {{SYSTEM_NAME}} source
#    Save output as inventory-actual.json

# 2. Compare against baseline manifest
python tools/doc-drift-checker.py manifest/manifest-{{DS_VERSION}}.json inventory-actual.json

# 3. Script reports missing/extra/drift, exits 0 (clean) or 1 (drift)
```

## The boundary with {{STUDIO_REPO}}

By design, this repo is **design-system only**. Screen creation + live prototyping live in the separate
**`{{STUDIO_REPO}}`**: composing {{SYSTEM_NAME}} into consumer screens (the consumer file
`{{CONSUMER_FIGMA_KEY}}`), the UX definition that precedes it (flows/IA/specs), and coded prototypes for
usability testing.

**Handoff across the boundary:** `ds-steward` publishes {{SYSTEM_NAME}} + updates `skills/` + `manifest/`;
the studio context consumes the published library to build. A {{SYSTEM_NAME}} gap found while building a
screen comes back here as a tokenization/component request — `ds-steward` + `component-designer` build
and publish it, the screen context waits or stubs. `DESIGN.md` is shared ground truth both repos read.

